**Providing search input code**

I'm looking at a few code snippets for a search input feature. First, in the plain JavaScript version, I’m creating a search field with an event listener that searches when a button is clicked or when the Enter key is pressed. Then for the React variant, I’m using state to manage the input value and a handler for the search operation. I’ll include an SVG for the magnifying glass inside the button, as it seems like the clear choice for interactivity. Let's keep it concise!
[/Thinking]

Here’s a simple, accessible way to place a clickable SVG search icon on the right side of an input and call a function when it’s clicked (and on Enter).

Plain HTML/CSS/JS
- Uses a button with an inline SVG so it’s keyboard- and screen-reader-friendly.
- Adds right padding to the input so text doesn’t sit under the icon.

HTML:
<div class="input-with-icon">
  <input type="text" id="search" placeholder="Search..." />
  <button type="button" class="icon-btn" aria-label="Search" id="searchBtn">
    <!-- Magnifying glass SVG -->
    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27A6.471 6.471 0 0 0 16 9.5 6.5 6.5 0 1 0 9.5 16c1.61 0 3.09-.59 4.23-1.57l.27.28v.79l5 4.99L20.49 19l-4.99-5zm-6 0C7.01 14 5 11.99 5 9.5S7.01 5 9.5 5 14 7.01 14 9.5 11.99 14 9.5 14z"></path>
    </svg>
  </button>
</div>

CSS:
.input-with-icon {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  width: 280px; /* adjust as needed */
}
.input-with-icon input {
  width: 100%;
  padding-right: 2.5rem; /* space for the icon */
  box-sizing: border-box;
  height: 40px;
}
.input-with-icon .icon-btn {
  position: absolute;
  right: 0.5rem;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  height: 32px;
  width: 32px;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  border: none;
  background: transparent;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: inherit;
}
.input-with-icon .icon-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #2684ff;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

JS:
const input = document.getElementById('search');
const btn = document.getElementById('searchBtn');

function onClick() {
  const q = input.value.trim();
  if (!q) return;
  // Your function logic here
  console.log('Searching for:', q);
}

btn.addEventListener('click', onClick);
input.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.key === 'Enter') onClick();
});

React version
function SearchInput({ onSearch }) {
  const [q, setQ] = React.useState('');

  const handleSearch = () => {
    const value = q.trim();
    if (!value) return;
    onSearch?.(value);
  };

  return (
    <div className="input-with-icon">
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Search..."
        value={q}
        onChange={(e) => setQ(e.target.value)}
        onKeyDown={(e) => e.key === 'Enter' && handleSearch()}
      />
      <button type="button" className="icon-btn" aria-label="Search" onClick={handleSearch}>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27A6.471 6.471 0 0 0 16 9.5 6.5 6.5 0 1 0 9.5 16c1.61 0 3.09-.59 4.23-1.57l.27.28v.79l5 4.99L20.49 19l-4.99-5zm-6 0C7.01 14 5 11.99 5 9.5S7.01 5 9.5 5 14 7.01 14 9.5 11.99 14 9.5 14z"></path>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

Use the same CSS from the plain example. You can replace the SVG with your own icon; keeping it inside a button ensures it’s clickable and accessible.

